Setup: Serena 1.7.0, Claude Code, Windows 11, Gleam 1.17.0.

In a Gleam project, `find_referencing_symbols` misses a caller when the calling function has the same name as the target.

```gleam
// src/probe/b.gleam
pub fn run() -> Int { 1 }

// src/probe/a.gleam
import probe/b
pub fn run() -> Int { b.run() }

// src/probe/c.gleam
import probe/b
pub fn run() -> Int { b.run() }
```

`find_referencing_symbols(name_path="run", relative_path="src/probe/b.gleam")` returns `a.run` but not `c.run`. Asking the Gleam LSP directly (`textDocument/references`) returns both.

Looks like the import check in `request_referencing_symbols` (`solidlsp/ls.py`): once the self-reference is seen, any later reference whose containing symbol has the same name and kind is skipped as an import. The Gleam LSP includes the declaration in the list, so same-named callers listed after it are dropped. The same layout in Rust and Python returns all callers.
